; The bool-if lint's fixture: `(if ok true false)` answers its
; condition unchanged, so the `if` adds nothing. The control below
; pins what the rule does not cover - a condition that is a call
; rather than a bare name stays silent, because the squiggle must
; spell what the message quotes.
(:: isYes (-> Bool Bool))
(fn (isYes ok)
  (if ok
    true
    false))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (if (isYes true)
    1
    0))
